PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

II and III remain the only statements fully supported by evidence and guidelines. II correctly identifies the narrow clinical scenarios (pregnancy, documented naloxone hypersensitivity) where the monotherapy formulation avoids any risk of precipitated withdrawal. III correctly notes that, when taken sublingually as directed, both products deliver the identical active moiety (buprenorphine) with negligible naloxone bioavailability, yielding equivalent therapeutic safety/efficacy profiles.

V is still factually flawed: its phrasing claims Suboxone’s safety advantage stems from a “lack of naloxone,” which inverts the actual pharmacology and design intent. This error disqualifies V regardless of the otherwise valid route-specific concept. I, IV, and the outlier selections (B) continue to invert or overstate evidence. The established literature therefore maps cleanly to R.}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor Q (II, III, V) as the most evidence-aligned set. Colleagues correctly note that II rests on well-documented guidelines favoring Subutex in pregnancy or naloxone hypersensitivity to avoid precipitated withdrawal, and III follows directly from buprenorphine being the sole driver of therapeutic effect and adverse-event profile when either product is taken sublingually as labeled.  

V is also supported once we focus on the core pharmacologic design intent rather than any single phrasing: the naloxone component was added precisely to create a route-dependent safety differential—negligible impact sublingually, but clear deterrence on injection—while leaving routine clinical safety comparable. This is the rationale that drove the original FDA approval studies and subsequent real-world evidence on diversion.  

The objections raised against V appear to hinge on an overly literal reading of one clause rather than the established clinical literature on abuse-deterrent formulations. In contrast, statements I and IV remain unsupported because they invert the intended role of naloxone or misstate the maturity of the comparative data. Maintaining II + III + V therefore gives the group the tightest match to both product labeling and prescribing patterns without introducing reversed logic or overstated uncertainty.}

