The majority view (now 8/10) correctly isolates only II and III as fully evidence-supported. II aligns with long-established guidelines (ACOG, product labeling) that explicitly prefer Subutex monotherapy in pregnancy or naloxone hypersensitivity to avoid precipitated withdrawal. III follows directly from pharmacology: when either product is taken sublingually as directed, naloxone bioavailability is negligible and buprenorphine alone drives identical therapeutic safety/efficacy.  

V remains disqualified by its explicit factual reversal (“lack of naloxone” for Suboxone), regardless of route-specific intent. I inverts the abuse-deterrence rationale; IV misstates the state of knowledge. The narrow, accurate selection is therefore still R.}

