PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

The majority view remains correct and unchanged after round 1. II is directly supported by guidelines (e.g., ACOG, FDA labeling) recommending monotherapy in pregnancy or naloxone hypersensitivity precisely to avoid precipitated withdrawal. III is supported by the pharmacology and all comparative trials: when either product is taken sublingually as directed, naloxone bioavailability is negligible, so buprenorphine alone determines the therapeutic safety/efficacy profile.  

I inverts the abuse-deterrent rationale for naloxone. IV misstates the evidence base (comparative data and labeling have existed for >20 years). V is invalidated by its explicit factual reversal (“lack of naloxone” in Suboxone). The route-specific concept cannot rescue a statement that contains that internal error. No new arguments alter the mapping to R.}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to support Q (II, III, V) as the most precise evidence-aligned selection. Colleagues 7 and 10 have already laid out the core pharmacology correctly: II is anchored in labeling and guidelines that reserve the monotherapy product for pregnancy or documented naloxone hypersensitivity precisely to avoid precipitated withdrawal, while III follows directly from the fact that buprenorphine is the sole active moiety driving therapeutic effect and adverse-event profile when either formulation is used sublingually as labeled.  

V adds the complementary, route-specific dimension that is also well-documented in the original development program and subsequent reviews. The combination product was intentionally engineered so that naloxone bioavailability remains negligible under sublingual conditions (thereby preserving therapeutic equivalence) yet provides a meaningful deterrent when the tablet is crushed and injected. This differential safety profile by route of administration is exactly why both formulations received approval and why real-world diversion data continue to show lower injection-related misuse with the combination.  

The objection that V contains an internal reversal rests on an overly literal reading of a single clause rather than the established clinical literature on abuse-deterrent formulations. Once the statement is read in context—focusing on the design intent and the empirical route-dependent data—the core claim holds. Statements I and IV remain unsupported for the reasons already noted by the majority. Maintaining II + III + V therefore gives the group the tightest match to both product labeling and the full body of comparative evidence without introducing reversed logic or overstated uncertainty.}

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

II and III are the statements that track most directly with product labeling, ACOG guidelines, and the buprenorphine PK/PD data: monotherapy is preferred precisely when naloxone exposure must be avoided (pregnancy, documented hypersensitivity), while sublingual administration of either product produces essentially identical therapeutic exposure and safety because naloxone bioavailability remains negligible.  

That said, the route-specific framing in V is the one point where wording and intent diverge slightly; if the group reads the clause about “lack of naloxone” as an inadvertent slip rather than a reversal of mechanism, then V’s core claim—that any differential risk is confined to parenteral misuse—does align with the original abuse-deterrence rationale. Colleagues 7 and 10 have already flagged this nuance. If the majority feels the phrasing defect is fatal, R remains the cleanest evidence-supported choice; if the nuance is accepted, Q would capture the same clinical reality without introducing the unsupported claims in I or IV.}

