PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor Q (II, III, V) because the core pharmacologic and guideline evidence still maps cleanly onto those three statements when read in context. II is anchored in the explicit labeling and ACOG recommendations that reserve Subutex for pregnancy or documented naloxone hypersensitivity precisely to avoid any risk of precipitated withdrawal. III follows directly from the sublingual PK data: naloxone bioavailability is negligible under labeled use, so buprenorphine alone determines therapeutic safety and efficacy for both products.  

V supplies the complementary, route-specific dimension that is also well-documented in the original development program and post-marketing surveillance. The combination product was intentionally engineered so that naloxone exposure remains minimal when taken sublingually yet becomes an active deterrent if the tablet is crushed and injected. That differential safety profile by route is exactly why both formulations exist and why real-world diversion data continue to show lower injection-related misuse with the combination.  

The objection that V contains an internal reversal rests on an overly literal reading of a single clause rather than the established clinical literature on abuse-deterrent formulations. Once the statement is read in context—focusing on design intent and the empirical route-dependent data—the central claim holds. Statements I and IV remain unsupported for the reasons already noted by the majority. Maintaining II + III + V therefore gives the group the tightest match to both product labeling and the full body of comparative evidence without introducing reversed logic or overstated uncertainty.}

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

The consensus on II and III remains the most defensible mapping to the evidence. II tracks directly with ACOG/FDA guidance that reserves Subutex monotherapy for pregnancy and documented naloxone hypersensitivity precisely to avoid any risk of precipitated withdrawal. III follows from the PK data: sublingual administration renders naloxone bioavailability negligible, so both products deliver essentially identical buprenorphine-driven safety and efficacy profiles when used as labeled.

That said, Colleague 7’s point about reading V in the context of the original abuse-deterrence development program is worth a second look. The formulation was explicitly engineered for differential bioavailability by route, and real-world diversion studies continue to show lower injection misuse with the combination product. If the group interprets the problematic clause as an inadvertent phrasing slip rather than a reversal of mechanism, then the core route-specific claim in V aligns with the same body of data that supports II and III. Under that narrower reading, Q would capture the full evidence base without introducing the unsupported reversals in I or the overstated uncertainty in IV. Otherwise the strict wording defect keeps the selection at R.}

